#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203686 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ea5e96d2-e6ae-5bf9-86a4-01e7a019a5e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14615" :question/problem "|  | $\\bigstar$ | $\\maltese$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined over the sets $\\{ 3, 4, 5 \\}$ and $\\{ \\bigstar, \\maltese \\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(4, \\bigstar)$\n\n- $(5, \\maltese)$\n\n- $(3, \\maltese)$" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A = \\{ a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_m \\}$ and $B = \\{ b_1, b_2, \\ldots b_n \\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m \\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element $r_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n![](images/88/8804000d9eeb8eeb08b97fb71d63df9b22b66ef9e4938117c0e4af775ff51f99.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4, \\bigstar)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $\\bigstar$-column.\n\n- The pair $(5, \\maltese)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $5$-row and the $\\maltese$-column.\n\n- The pair $(3, \\maltese)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $3$-row and the $\\maltese$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d3c62a4-9293-5033-89bb-695d511f1d20" :knowledge-point/title "Building an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4bcec8eb-63cf-5519-b7fa-a824ee54e943" :question/math-academy-id "e-14648" :question/problem "Consider the sets $A = \\{2,4,6\\}$ and $B= \\big\\{ \\{1,6\\}, \\{2,3\\}, \\mathbb{N}\\big\\},$ and the relation $R = \\{ (x,y) \\in A \\times B \\::\\: x \\in y \\}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $\\{1,6\\}$ | $\\{2,3\\}$ | $\\mathbb{N}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $6$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{1}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A = \\{ a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_m \\}$ and $B = \\{ b_1, b_2, \\ldots b_n \\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $r_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n![](images/6c/6c55c63f7cb03d7703a2b56ad15033ff5e01a6ae47543969206d6ba0be97f411.svg)\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with $x \\in y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x = 2,$ then the values $y=\\{2,3\\}$ and $y= \\mathbb{N}$ satisfy $x \\in y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,\\{2,3\\})$ and $(2,\\mathbb{N}),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x = 4,$ then the value $y = \\mathbb{N}$ satisfies $x \\in y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $\\big(4, \\mathbb{N} \\big),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x = 6,$ then the values $y=\\{1,6\\}$ and $y= \\mathbb{N}$ satisfy $x \\in y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,\\{1,6\\})$ and $(6,\\mathbb{N}),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $\\{1,6\\}$ | $\\{2,3\\}$ | $\\mathbb{N}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $6$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "05bbf2f9-cf55-5d11-b0d5-b647147f5b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14649" :question/problem "Consider the following relation on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: : \\: y = \\sin{x} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R \\subseteq A \\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- The set of first components contains all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}.$\nHence, the domain is $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Notice that $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq  1$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and it is continuous on $\\mathbb R.$ So, the set of second components contains all real numbers greater than or equal to $-1$ and less than or equal to $1$:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{y\\in \\mathbb R \\::\\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $\\{ y\\in \\mathbb R\\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277d0727-838a-539b-819b-da8d7ad8d022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413637} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5c613d-ce6f-5d2f-9d0d-b0d5c7bb9753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413640} {:answer/id #uuid "4593208d-f293-53fc-9281-392ec9c6d5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413643} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f8bb88-c52f-5544-9279-0cef54cf414b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413665} {:answer/id #uuid "fa660a92-6489-598b-aa9f-da0f6f998972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f8bb88-c52f-5544-9279-0cef54cf414b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5270f76-2521-5654-804b-ae6bd4a100ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93d6ac86-9246-53ae-8020-58de7dcc7070" :question/math-academy-id "q-213094" :question/problem "|  | $✠$ | $♣$ | $♠$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $✠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♣$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined on the set ${✠,♣,♠},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(✠,♣)$\n- $(♣,♠)$\n- $(♠,✠)$" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce382e1-19d7-5210-abc5-29008e719eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413639} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf02d47-944c-5078-b040-436ef4d1dd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413649} {:answer/id #uuid "9316338e-177e-596d-813f-c2b271e78ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413654} {:answer/id #uuid "d0194c58-f1d2-5a9f-83ca-8ef30dfdeae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413664} {:answer/id #uuid "e90085ef-ae69-595f-9edc-e65d95950ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0194c58-f1d2-5a9f-83ca-8ef30dfdeae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413664} :answer-field/id #uuid "90a2eff9-7291-52e7-a8a6-32507eaf07e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e24d1221-5380-50fb-b4e2-6e139d57675a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213100" :question/problem "|  | $3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined over the sets ${2,4,8}$ and ${3,6},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(4,3)$\n- $(8,6)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $3$-column.\n\n- The pair $(8,6)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $8$-row and the $6$-column.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $2$-row and the $3$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466eb5d1-24da-50af-bd41-cb8d9abdd975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413644} {:answer/id #uuid "59e97cad-ff57-55f5-a222-de1e52ad1d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413646} {:answer/id #uuid "e9efba66-4a87-5b2e-bcf2-4039dca75930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "466eb5d1-24da-50af-bd41-cb8d9abdd975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413644} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2cef3e-c33a-5bdd-84e2-893bff3d277b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38955579-4bbf-53c1-b94b-4e5c238942cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413641} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2ea2f9-26ff-5dd7-b8ee-8cbcf3527d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc2ea2f9-26ff-5dd7-b8ee-8cbcf3527d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413672} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed49e7d-084a-5fc0-a49e-dfb0548c2d83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5995eb11-b770-5524-a8df-8a5ee4a7dcbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413645} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc5897b-87bf-5bd8-a43b-dcae1e66f64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413648} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d14aef-3ac6-5e6a-ad47-cd83787d7a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413662} {:answer/id #uuid "da00a5c7-2c5d-5747-8de0-a8da60754681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d14aef-3ac6-5e6a-ad47-cd83787d7a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413662} :answer-field/id #uuid "faf9cf90-2f27-5a5a-8f4b-9a590b4a30eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8df7bc4f-b191-56e9-905d-73750dc43c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-213111" :question/problem "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is defined only for $x>1.$ So, the set of first components contains all real numbers greater than $1$:\n\n$$\n{x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}\n$$\n\nHence, the domain is ${x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}.$\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is continuous on $R,$ and has neither lower nor upper bounds. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the range is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d3c62a4-9293-5033-89bb-695d511f1d20" :knowledge-point/title "Building an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba7b9e6-076b-57c8-a49f-c2f233d245f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba7b9e6-076b-57c8-a49f-c2f233d245f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413642} :answer-field/id #uuid "0665a259-196d-5f49-8d9f-3fe90bc699e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88b74bce-a0de-5462-a031-109e23f8056b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88b74bce-a0de-5462-a031-109e23f8056b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413652} :answer-field/id #uuid "477031a5-7b05-568a-bf03-26feccc03a5b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd845b6-8d50-552b-a3b4-be72d5608d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbd845b6-8d50-552b-a3b4-be72d5608d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413671} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b347a0-a9d6-50d2-b46b-77ca83ad92a9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c6da89-1bf9-5c09-a900-2173a27faf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c6da89-1bf9-5c09-a900-2173a27faf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413638} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb56abab-e923-5821-a5e5-dad20e12f612" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413686} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c65cad00-e92b-5864-82b7-3093ffea2302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c65cad00-e92b-5864-82b7-3093ffea2302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413661} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f099d4-13c7-5012-be7b-31df44a55312" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "774de0b5-5c02-58cd-a5ae-6ac59d12337a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213114" :question/problem "Consider the set $B={2,4,6}$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $4$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |\n| $6$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2,4,6$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(2,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d3c62a4-9293-5033-89bb-695d511f1d20" :knowledge-point/title "Building an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4df8e9-1089-52a8-8fc4-5cb8c93337a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4df8e9-1089-52a8-8fc4-5cb8c93337a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413659} :answer-field/id #uuid "5596bbe9-cd58-5494-884b-6a7498912540" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413675} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5492ab-8752-5496-97c7-a39039240230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5492ab-8752-5496-97c7-a39039240230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413658} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce07eb8-fd06-5401-9545-1a8f1505f812" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bff79e1-4d90-5045-88dc-0027149e07b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bff79