86413650} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7433bc2-749d-5ea5-a463-f66c626a5580" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413687} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cff8e13-d990-55d5-bfc3-2b19181defd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cff8e13-d990-55d5-bfc3-2b19181defd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413653} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f54a7f-d74c-52b0-87da-05b966c210d9" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9e513d0-e8df-5b01-bf4e-b611860d77a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-213121" :question/problem "Consider the sets $A={1,4,8}$ and $B={0,6,12},$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $4$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |\n| $8$ | {{field-6}} | {{field-7}} | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=0,6,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,0),$ $(1,6),$ and $(1,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=0,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,0)$ and $(4,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0ca2aa-12dd-5918-af70-866246d33858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413656} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe1f8e2-c22c-5deb-ae97-60ecf2e07b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{0}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413660} {:answer/id #uuid "c73cb8fb-53f9-5ca0-97dc-f93263818489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ca2aa-12dd-5918-af70-866246d33858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413656} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8591feb-deca-5fea-9d46-aabf66584f7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ad9036-c5da-521f-8faf-a4fa68cfc8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413636} {:answer/id #uuid "b580ba27-bd08-5254-8f93-b9a06915a0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413657} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e06782-2fba-5bec-8b7f-010640ecc160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{0}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ad9036-c5da-521f-8faf-a4fa68cfc8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413636} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d19dd9-a43f-5b7b-baac-57d1d370cbbc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da7f544c-d9de-529a-84b0-118cccccae2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-213423" :question/problem "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y={e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- The set of first components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the domain is $R.$\n\n- Notice that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R,$ it is continuous on $R,$ and has no upper bound. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers greater than zero:\n\n$$\n{y\\in R\\,:\\,y>0}={R}^{+}\n$$\n\nHence, the range is ${R}^{+}.$"}]}
